<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Averroes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1126">1126</a>–<a href="/years/1198">1198</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Ibn Rushd</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Averroes, or Ibn Rushd, was an Andalusian philosopher, physician and judge of the twelfth century who served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a>. He wrote influential commentaries on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>, which were later translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and shaped European scholasticism. He is one of the most important intellectual figures associated with the Almohad Caliphate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/averroes.jpg" alt="Averroes" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_di_bonaiuto,_apoteosi_di_san_tommaso_d%27aquino,_11_averro%C3%A8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
